% ============================================================================
% map-type — three named types of map and what each actually shows
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% Not every map shows the same thing: a political map shows human-made
% boundaries, a physical map shows the natural landscape, and a
% topographic map shows the shape of the land itself. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? map_type(political, $D)
%       % shows_boundaries_between_countries_states_counties_and_other_political_units
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `map_type(type, description)` carrying the source citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a map-related term that is not one of these three. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the type it
% names:
%
%     ? map_type($T, shows_the_shape_of_earths_surface)
%       % topographic
%
% The three map types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type           description                                                                    quote (verbatim)
%     -------------  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     political       shows_boundaries_between_countries_states_counties_and_other_political_units    "Political maps show boundaries between countries, states, counties, and other political units."
%     physical        shows_the_natural_landscape_features_of_earth                                   "Physical maps are designed to show the natural landscape features of Earth."
%     topographic     shows_the_shape_of_earths_surface                                               "Topographic maps are reference maps that show the shape of Earth's surface."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Geology.com's "Types of Maps" article,
% which introduces each of these three map types under its own section
% heading, each with a complete defining sentence. WebFetch-verified
% before writing (twice — the second pass specifically confirmed each
% sentence's exact wording, the section heading it appears under, and
% that no additional clause is attached as part of the same defining
% thought). `trust consensus` — Geology.com is a reputable secondary
% reference, the same tier the sibling `rock-type.adj` (in `geology/`,
% a sibling directory) uses for some of its non-USGS citations. A map
% type that is NOT one of these three — "weather maps", a real category
% the same page also covers, but one whose own section describes its
% uses across several sentences without ever stating a single, complete
% defining sentence the way the three tabled here do — is deliberately
% NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a
% description. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table map_type {
    columns type, description

    row (political, shows_boundaries_between_countries_states_counties_and_other_political_units)
    row (physical, shows_the_natural_landscape_features_of_earth)
    row (topographic, shows_the_shape_of_earths_surface)

    source "Political maps show boundaries between countries, states, counties, and other political units."
    locator "https://geology.com/maps/types-of-maps/"
    trust consensus
}
